course: dictionary
lesson: 15690
title: Tinned: food in a can, or covered in tin?
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: «Tinned» is a word you will see on food labels and in shops. Tinned food is food sealed in a metal can to keep it fresh for a long time. «We ate tinned tomatoes on toast for tea.» The word tinned tells you the tomatoes came in a can.
explain: In the US people say «canned» instead. So «canned beans» means the same as «tinned beans». Tinned can also mean covered with a thin layer of tin, as in «The wire was tinned, so it did not rust.» Here the wire is not food at all.
explain: To work out which meaning is right, look at the words around tinned. If you see food, fish, fruit or soup, it means sealed in a can. If you see wire, steel or copper, it means covered with a thin layer of tin.
fact: Tinned means sealed in a metal can to keep food fresh for a long time.
fact: In the US, people say «canned» for the same kind of food.
fact: Tinned can also mean covered with a thin layer of tin, as in tinned steel or tinned copper wire.
fact: Tinned food was invented in the early 1800s to feed soldiers and sailors.
fact: Tinned beans, tomatoes, tuna and peaches are cheap and last a long time, so food banks use them.
exercise: «Mum put tinned tomatoes in the soup.» What does tinned mean here? | Cooked and eaten the same day | *Sealed in a metal can to keep fresh | Frozen in a plastic bag | Covered with a thin layer of tin
exercise: «The wire was tinned, so it did not rust.» What does tinned mean here? | *Covered with a thin layer of tin | Very cold | Painted bright red | Sealed in a can
exercise: «We kept a shelf full of tinned peaches for emergencies.» Find and copy the word that tells you the peaches came in a can. | emergencies | *tinned | full | shelf
test: «The food bank was full of tinned beans and tuna.» Where would you most likely find tinned food? | On the top of a mountain | *At a food bank or in a shop | In a bird's nest | Inside a tree
test: In the US, people say «canned» instead of tinned. Which word means the same as tinned food? | Fresh bread | *Canned food | Frozen food | Dried flowers
exam: Sailors on long voyages in the early 1800s ate tinned food. Why was tinned food useful to them? | It could be eaten only on land | It had to be eaten the same day | It was always served hot | *It stayed fresh for a long time
